===Etymology 2===
From {{inh|en|enm|drink}}, {{m|enm|drinke}} (also as {{m|enm|drinche}}, {{m|enm|drunch}}), from {{inh|en|ang|drynċ}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*drunkiz}}, {{m|gem-pro|*drankiz}}. Compare {{cog|nl|drank}}.

====Noun====
{{en-noun|~}}

# {{senseid|en|Q40050}}A [[beverage]].
#: {{ux|en|I’d like another '''drink''' please.}}
# {{lb|en|uncountable}} Drinks in general; something to drink.
#* {{RQ:King James Version|book=Matthew|chapter=25|verse=35|passage=For I was an hungred, and ye gave me meat: I was thirsty, and ye gave me '''drink'''}}
# A type of [[beverage]] (usually mixed).
#: {{ux|en|My favourite '''drink''' is the White Russian.}}
# A ([[serve]]d) alcoholic [[beverage]].
#: {{ux|en|Can I buy you a '''drink'''?}}
# The action of [[drinking]], especially with the verbs ''[[take]]'' or ''[[have]]''.
#: {{ux|en|He was about to take a '''drink''' from his root beer.}}
# [[alcohol|Alcoholic]] [[beverage]]s in general.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1935|author=[https://www.goodreads.com/author/show/288354.George_Goodchild George Goodchild]
|title=Death on the Centre Court|chapter=1
|passage=She mixed furniture with the same fatal profligacy as she mixed '''drinks''', and this outrageous contact between things which were intended by Nature to be kept poles apart gave her an inexpressible thrill.}}
#* {{quote-song|en|year=1995|artist=w:Bruce McCulloch|album=Shame-Based Man|track=12|title=Daddy's on the '''Drink'''|url=https://genius.com/Bruce-mcculloch-daddys-on-the-drink-lyrics|passage=The face of work is a drunk man in the same chair, chewing on the same bone for five thousand nights. The face of work is a, coffee cup in hand, frustrated: "You don't get it. They all don't get it. You don't understand, man." Daddy's on the '''drink''' again.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=w:Blake Bailey|title='Tennessee Williams,' by John Lahr &#91;print version: Theatrical victory of art over life, ''International New York Times'', 18 November 2014, p.&nbsp;13&#93;|url=http://www.nytimes.com/2014/11/16/books/review/tennessee-williams-by-john-lahr.html|work=w:The New York Times|date=14 November 2014|passage={{...}} she was indeed Amanda in the flesh: a doughty chatterbox from Ohio who adopted the manner of a Southern belle and eschewed both '''drink''' and sex to the greatest extent possible.}}
# A [[standard drink]].
#: {{ux|en|A '''drink''' of wine is about 5 ounces}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1963|title=Vital and Health Statistics: Programs and collection procedures|page=125|text=And when (SUBJECT) was 55, would you say (he/she) drank more than, less than, or about 2 to 3 '''drinks''' a day?}}
# {{lb|en|colloquial|with ''the''}} Any body of water.
#: {{ux|en|If he doesn't pay off the mafia, he’ll wear cement shoes to the bottom of the '''drink'''!}}
#* {{quote-book
|year=1996
|author=John French+
|title=A Drop in the Ocean: Dramatic Accounts of Aircrew Saved From the Sea
|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=lgSSAwAAQBAJ&pg=PT99
|isbn=9781473813809
|page=99
|publisher=Pen and Sword
|1=en
|passage=When in mid-Channel the speed slowed and I was informed by A.C. Russell that another dinghy had been spotted. This turned out to contain a Canadian fighter pilot who had been in the '''drink''' for three days and was in rather a bad way. He said he had seen all the aircraft flying over in the two days before D-Day and since, but no one had sighted him.}}
#* {{quote-book
|year=2011
|author=Levi Johnston
|title=Deer in the Headlights: My Life in Sarah Palin's Crosshairs
|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=g9eNWzfPx8IC&pg=PT34
|isbn=9781451651676
|page=34
|publisher=Simon and Schuster
|1=en
|passage=In seconds, we went from sitting in a boat to threading ice-cold water. I wasn't wearing a life jacket and am not the best paddler, but there I was, in the '''drink''', splashing around.}}
#* {{quote-book
|year=2012
|author=Jack R. Myers
|title=Shot at and Missed: Recollections of a World War II Bombardier
|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=93PZi5CSZ0AC&pg=PT31
|isbn=9780806184890
|page=31
|publisher=University of Oklahoma Press
|1=en
|passage=If the planes couldn't make it, they would go in the '''drink''', eject their rubber lifeboats, inflate them, climb in, and pray for the Navy to pick them up before the Germans did.}}
# {{lb|en|Australia|figurative}} A [[downpour]]; a [[cloudburst]]; a [[rainstorm]]; a [[deluge]]; a lot of [[rain]].
#* {{quote-av|en|speaker=Nate Byrne|title=[[:w:News Breakfast|News Breakfast]]|season=2023|number=74|publisher=[[:w:ABC News (Australia)|ABC News]]|date=13 April 2023|at=07:56 am (UTC+10/AEST)|location=Melbourne, Australia|text=Now this is going to bring some huge totals of rainfall with it—200 to 400 millimetres with it—and along with that, these winds—gusts to 275 kilometres an hour near the cyclone &#91;[[:w:Cyclone Ilsa|Cyclone Ilsa]]&#93; core—and that's a real concern. That's very destructive winds and it's going to carry this inertia and the rain with it well inland. And we're likely going to be talking about a cyclone all the way through Friday as it slowly weakens, eventually washing that moisture out into a front going through the south. It means the southeast is getting a '''drink''' but [[Western Australia|W.A.]]'s northwest really copping it, individual totals significantly higher than what you're seeing here [on the weather map].}}

=====Usage notes=====
* A plainer term than more elevated term {{m|en|beverage}}. '''Beverage''' is of French origin, while ''drink'' is of Old English origin, and this stylistic difference by origin is common; see {{w|list of English words with dual French and Anglo-Saxon variations}}.
* In the sense of ''any body of water'' the term is often associated with (a threat of) [[drowning]].

